{"name":"Axis","hex":"#bab6cb","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Axis","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Cervidae – hog deer."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The alliance (in effect before and during World War II) of Germany, Italy, Japan, and several minor allied countries, which opposed the Allies. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Axis"}],"entries":[{"title":"Line (geometry)","blocks":[{"text":"In geometry, a straight line, usually abbreviated line, is an infinitely long object with no width, depth, or curvature. It is a special case of a curve and an idealisation of such physical objects as a straightedge, a taut string, or a ray of light. Lines are spaces of dimension one, which may be embedded in spaces of dimension two, three, or higher. The word line may also refer, in everyday life, to a line segment, which is a part of a line delimited by two points."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Gerade.svg/330px-Gerade.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Line_(geometry)","wikidata":"Q37105"},{"title":"Coordinate system","blocks":[{"text":"In geometry, a coordinate system is a system that uses one or more numbers, or coordinates, to uniquely determine and standardize the position of the points or other geometric elements on a manifold such as Euclidean space. The coordinates are not interchangeable; they are commonly distinguished by their position in an ordered tuple, or by a label, such as in \"the x-coordinate\". The coordinates are taken to be real numbers in elementary mathematics, but may be complex numbers or elements of a more abstract system such as a commutative ring. The use of a coordinate system allows problems in geometry to be translated into problems about numbers and vice versa; this is the basis of analytic geometry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/3D_Spherical.svg/330px-3D_Spherical.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coordinate_system","wikidata":"Q11210"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Great_circle_hemispheres.png/500px-Great_circle_hemispheres.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Great_circle_hemispheres.png/500px-Great_circle_hemispheres.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A great circle divides the sphere in two equal hemispheres, while also satisfying the \"no curvature\" property.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreat_circle_hemispheres.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Parabolic_coordinates_3D.png/500px-Parabolic_coordinates_3D.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Parabolic_coordinates_3D.png/500px-Parabolic_coordinates_3D.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coordinate surfaces of the three-dimensional paraboloidal coordinates.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParabolic_coordinates_3D.png"}],"sounds":{"Axis":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-Axis.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/96/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-Axis.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-Axis.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/96/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_